A||N||1||stimpy||Here's a weird distributor issue.||||5||
Z||000000||stimpy||04-23-2001||01:33 PM||||My car has been alittle sluggish in the mid-range (between 2-4k rpm) and since I recently cleaned my engine, I figured I'd check for moisture in the distributor cap. I pulled the cap and it was dry, but I looked at the pickups and noticed that the pickup for #1 cylinder was turned 90 degrees. It almost looked like someone twisted it with pliers, but there were no marks on it like you would get from pliers.  How in the hell did my car run at all?  I did put new cap and rotor on for insurance, and it does seem to run a lot better.<P>Just weird though.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||04-23-2001||02:47 PM||||Lousy GM QC- probably came that way from the factory  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Actually, if you didn't buy your car new, and/or if you had Wal*Mart do a "tune-up" along with their last oil change on your car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, I'd bet somebody along the way <B>bent</B> it.<P>Even though there's no plier marks.  Maybe they used a "proper" tool, like a plug gapper, that didn't leave any marks.  How do I know so much, when I haven't even seen it?  I don't.  I'm just guessing.  I <B>have</B> seen some pretty nasty things done to distributor pole pieces, though, in trying to change the pickup coil w/o pulling the distributor.  <P>By the way, the pickup coil doesn't care which particular "finger" on the pole piece is at a particular location.  It just gets a pulse every 60 degrees - it would probably  <B>prefer</B>, however, that all six of the "fingers" line up at the same time.  That produces the strongest signal in the coil.  At least, that's what it looks like to Stu.  5 out of 6 ain't bad, so it's no surprise that it runs.          ||||reg||3
